#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1220ac85-068f-5685-ad04-7914a59641ba" :knowledge-point/title "Recovering the Joint PDF from a Joint CDF"}] :question/id #uuid "4af79cd3-8c50-533f-a744-3784a71a15dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10117" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n![](images/74/747403765cdc8cdcdad6913f9253d75dc947aab12520d009e25fcd9bd24ad3ae.svg)\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\; y \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x, y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial^2}{\\partial x \\partial y} F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $y \\geq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}(1-{e}^{-y}))) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{e}^{-y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{-y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y) = 0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n![](images/ce/cedf8a240fb8e0642642d5f39a4b174219e9b7c0283a5a3a469f431815e9909b.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188066 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aae311f3-6d07-5104-aa55-5d76b986510a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal CDF Using a Joint CDF"}] :question/id #uuid "731d3a2d-4853-52e4-9c86-3654dc66adab" :question/math-academy-id "e-10436" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y) \\in [0,1] \\times [0,2],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy}{8}(2x^2+y).\n$$\n\nFind $F_X(x),$ the marginal CDF of $X,$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_X(x) = F(x,\\infty) = \\lim\\limits_{y \\to \\infty} F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\leq x\\leq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & =F(x,\\infty ) \\\\  & =F(x,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{x(2)}{8}(2{x}^{2}+(2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{8}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}+x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188212 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f96f4b28-61d0-5c7e-b779-104245fe26d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from Marginal CDFs for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "b48c754c-18c0-575f-bc6a-54f6fbdcec62" :question/math-academy-id "e-9223" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n![](images/d9/d978077685f19e7a958f69c84002e2db1d1ad555a5e4c95bf4236557a5c51cb5.svg)\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x \\geq 1$ and $y\\geq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs $F_X(x)$ and $F_Y(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = F_X(x) \\cdot F_Y (y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x \\geq 1$ and $y \\geq 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{x})(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}+\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n![](images/6e/6e5640b83f8602ed4381663f220801276880a024061eb26912f7a28a7f1ffbda.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1220ac85-068f-5685-ad04-7914a59641ba" :knowledge-point/title "Recovering the Joint PDF from a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096fe8eb-0f73-5dcd-9283-5609e6cf6c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526521} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7b246b-a91d-5397-9b3e-492593bcf3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526572} {:answer/id #uuid "9ffd77dc-caa5-5a7b-9077-2effd6a99afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526812} {:answer/id #uuid "c936279a-3486-5ccb-b9e0-fffe1ccea34f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526891} {:answer/id #uuid "f30d0020-1a54-5639-ab8d-73a8529f890a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ffd77dc-caa5-5a7b-9077-2effd6a99afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526812} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ab855f-f5a7-5070-a185-6c452a9551a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74fc0efc-8b80-569c-9c95-98f9405e5eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-159311" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)({y}^{2}-1)}{x{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(x-2)({y}^{2}-1)}{x{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-2}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-2}{x}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1220ac85-068f-5685-ad04-7914a59641ba" :knowledge-point/title "Recovering the Joint PDF from a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236a2d72-bb0b-50be-bba3-e4d52248a15f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526581} {:answer/id #uuid "4b66b758-0826-5108-9ac0-ade8eefbc63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526657} {:answer/id #uuid "71dd7185-fad4-53e7-b17c-bea3ed88b7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526723} {:answer/id #uuid "b08f83bf-773c-562d-b48f-e6657ce17368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526838} {:answer/id #uuid "d77ff831-4178-5cb0-8b9c-9a40136d4cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b08f83bf-773c-562d-b48f-e6657ce17368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526838} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c66bcf-f118-5a15-8c75-354e4b9c987f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcac8514-54e9-5652-a542-ae9e241a741a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159315" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f96f4b28-61d0-5c7e-b779-104245fe26d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from Marginal CDFs for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ded94d4-f6db-5cd8-9fbd-892a2aec9b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526693} {:answer/id #uuid "818e2fbb-826c-599b-9c21-644aa5a7adec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526762} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b757df-503d-509d-9bb1-a13dcd28aa28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526814} {:answer/id #uuid "ba331054-dad2-50ac-98dd-c8c0997ac923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526858} {:answer/id #uuid "eee91643-fab4-507a-be86-e3d0341b84c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0b757df-503d-509d-9bb1-a13dcd28aa28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526814} :answer-field/id #uuid "b432ba9e-dcac-5433-8ff8-190ee8bb1ea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18e3d97c-1951-59bd-b3e7-0c892c88f04a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159316" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{y}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})(1-\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f96f4b28-61d0-5c7e-b779-104245fe26d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from Marginal CDFs for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a3f1677-b272-57cb-bfa3-3f5d3ab81a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526525} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8acf17-693a-53fb-9034-d0608ef3c3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526534} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc14b6f-032f-51fe-a8fa-be5878a1d00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526662} {:answer/id #uuid "68a4acc5-d960-59ae-98ec-17c1c59437b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526712} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb7fa80-1aa3-58ec-a7a7-78c251d5440b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dc14b6f-032f-51fe-a8fa-be5878a1d00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526662} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d562ef-bf77-5294-b08b-2567310b5dfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3af2400-05b8-5370-91fa-551e055c25be" :question/math-academy-id "q-159318" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & y>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\text{ or }y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}, & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f96f4b28-61d0-5c7e-b779-104245fe26d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from Marginal CDFs for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f14ebf1-a6a5-5a1c-bceb-b94a30140ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}-2{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526570} {:answer/id #uuid "88fdae8b-27cd-5d83-8742-cb8138b1a6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526776} {:answer/id #uuid "c717cb6b-8e7f-518f-9495-eec458502f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526885} {:answer/id #uuid "e402e686-3ff6-5db1-a2cc-d914d442a9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526951} {:answer/id #uuid "e95e6242-3b0b-565c-aa1a-1ac96cd9dddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e95e6242-3b0b-565c-aa1a-1ac96cd9dddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526965} :answer-field/id #uuid "4808f553-7313-53e6-9c9a-a373717e5764" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a66a770-559e-57c4-8545-74993f2211db" :question/math-academy-id "q-159320" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{1-y}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}})(1-{e}^{1-y}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f96f4b28-61d0-5c7e-b779-104245fe26d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from Marginal CDFs for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15aa0740-bb6f-50d4-a937-2834a1c7e53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{x{e}^{-2y}}{2+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526547} {:answer/id #uuid "9664a9c8-6635-5edb-95e2-cc6d28ff1c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526797} {:answer/id #uuid "b69b91e9-4e20-5e6f-b2c3-445a82748c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1+x}(1-{e}^{-2y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526854} {:answer/id #uuid "e34a28f2-4fe3-5a59-b680-84b2b53ea3c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{x}{2+x}-{e}^{-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526948} {:answer/id #uuid "f54531dc-c152-57ba-b9ee-ccc647ffc034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2y}}{2+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9664a9c8-6635-5edb-95e2-cc6d28ff1c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526797} :answer-field/id #uuid "55ed3e7c-fab6-5103-98c8-80e32b0d59cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a27194f1-04ae-54c8-8fbb-2f24bc3ed222" :question/math-academy-id "q-159322" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2+x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-2y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{2+x})(1-{e}^{-2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y}), & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f96f4b28-61d0-5c7e-b779-104245fe26d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from Marginal CDFs for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ac938f-f798-5038-bab5-fa297deba0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526502} {:answer/id #uuid "03d36e20-40e6-5b80-9c0d-67f894b58f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{24}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526509} {:answer/id #uuid "254d8e8c-0380-5000-b8d0-a054397414b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526585} {:answer/id #uuid "b225b894-490f-581e-bce4-4a2a39058d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526843} {:answer/id #uuid "e29f4e96-d27a-5c57-bc4c-2abd5d1dceda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b225b894-490f-581e-bce4-4a2a39058d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526843} :answer-field/id #uuid "35729131-6ecd-51d1-af5b-b92e2a8eb812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1859419-9341-5ec9-877c-8ef2835f890f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159323" :question/problem "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}, & x\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 2,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}})(1-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1220ac85-068f-5685-ad04-7914a59641ba" :knowledge-point/title "Recovering the Joint PDF from a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca9b839-cb5a-5b4b-bc58-e09b93f3700e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526528} {:answer/id #uuid "18bd9257-411d-5063-94ed-ecd8343134ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526556} {:answer/id #uuid "3fcd8e4f-e442-55ec-acdd-e2642ab81430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526628} {:answer/id #uuid "a0127b97-00fd-5391-a92c-e6076c707eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526813} {:answer/id #uuid "cdab7955-73b1-53f6-a3ff-c803c784a336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}x(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca9b839-cb5a-5b4b-bc58-e09b93f3700e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526528} :answer-field/id #uuid "e29738f2-3cd0-58f4-93b4-014da2a887b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "433f0ce6-f1e1-52cc-bff5-350654ecc1ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-159327" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{48}xy(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>4 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{48}xy(x+y))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+x\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+2y\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}(2x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1220ac85-068f-5685-ad04-7914a59641ba" :knowledge-point/title "Recovering the Joint PDF from a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "717d95b7-fa98-5ce7-87da-3b5f14244fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526722} {:answer/id #uuid "85f0574f-2eb3-5a2e-a9b4-0e626bc0fe9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}{y}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526771} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad206e5-9b08-5529-8514-60481a8f3392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{5/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526781} {:answer/id #uuid "a5562678-6941-5fda-a0d2-04f3395f2ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}{y}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526819} {:answer/id #uuid "da74a110-efe7-5877-a2c6-a01a07f76757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "717d95b7-fa98-5ce7-87da-3b5f14244fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526722} :answer-field/id #uuid "135ebc58-1d65-54f4-8dbd-b35d2b714a66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2603d8b7-b055-59f9-a9de-a5bcf92cb12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159330" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>1 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1220ac85-068f-5685-ad04-7914a59641ba" :knowledge-point/title "Recovering the Joint PDF from a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "575f89b2-b860-5bf6-94c2-b2386d5174b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{3{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526682} {:answer/id #uuid "ac47ec72-2d41-5b5a-b068-bbdef9147797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{3{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526834} {:answer/id #uuid "dd56e164-faac-55a7-ae60-d5adf3110db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16{x}^{3}}{9{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526935} {:answer/id #uuid "fa973044-c950-5daa-a2a0-d7518f5a10c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527000} {:answer/id #uuid "fe148dd0-c4b8-52c9-9a1c-2693969bc24e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{9{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa973044-c950-5daa-a2a0-d7518f5a10c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527000} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bb56fd2-9bf7-57d4-98c0-5fd980b76e4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f97149b0-2afa-55ad-bcc3-a7b6ba9e10a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159331" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}({y}^{2}-1)}{3{y}^{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y>2 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>1,\\,y>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{4{x}^{2}({y}^{2}-1)}{3{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3{y}^{3}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aae311f3-6d07-5104-aa55-5d76b986510a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal CDF Using a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac8b5d3-4035-5dd3-a5dd-ebf6500b19f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526526} {:answer/id #uuid "1319139a-d5dc-5725-94ac-d3880fa8552c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526540} {:answer/id #uuid "39126990-5fa4-5f41-bc2f-d291064aff94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526610} {:answer/id #uuid "94939d5f-6f1b-5dd9-ac7a-3a24e4324e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526793} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe28192-dd92-5dbb-b0a6-33f806a9c962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fe28192-dd92-5dbb-b0a6-33f806a9c962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526811} :answer-field/id #uuid "021bcd34-e94e-5e91-97f6-6e6c378cff60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02209bdf-5c25-5d71-9f0c-90055349806b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159335" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{xy-y}{x}, & x\\ge 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & x\\ge 1,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{xy-y}{x}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{y(x-1)}{x}) \\\\  & =y{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =y{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-0) \\\\  & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aae311f3-6d07-5104-aa55-5d76b986510a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal CDF Using a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1544ee01-f534-5f91-a989-82f6c4f8f30f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526544} {:answer/id #uuid "3ade37f7-6413-5d0c-aff6-f6ec6bccd435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526614} {:answer/id #uuid "3c40ec30-1fb8-58f2-8c9a-e6bd665ac483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526615} {:answer/id #uuid "a12ca628-2589-5dfb-9c58-44d876d2d743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526816} {:answer/id #uuid "a54b7301-a104-5d35-8c4d-e29e0811b8dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a54b7301-a104-5d35-8c4d-e29e0811b8dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526818} :answer-field/id #uuid "661c9719-3dbb-5353-ab1d-1d0798143c7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86ba6c5d-d640-5e57-88b7-9b3f13232b7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159337" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y}{2\\pi }, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,y\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{X}(x),$ the marginal CDF of $X,$ for $0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=F(x,\\infty )={lim}_{y\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={lim}_{y\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y}{2\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{y\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aae311f3-6d07-5104-aa55-5d76b986510a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal CDF Using a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f08af6-0ae0-5946-af18-ac098c2a4f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526542} {:answer/id #uuid "89657f0a-62ba-5d95-a5f8-5203f8074307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526778} {:answer/id #uuid "9cde0cbd-f7f5-5917-89ab-3ac53ffe44b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526805} {:answer/id #uuid "d033fc14-e820-50e7-b101-809826765d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526904} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c11f33-2f49-5bf1-984d-6f4110492807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89657f0a-62ba-5d95-a5f8-5203f8074307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526778} :answer-field/id #uuid "d097562d-4f89-51da-8330-c4683f7fa9ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "856af2a4-8ad4-5acc-a980-a089f44a32f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-159344" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(1,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1){y}^{2}+(1)^{2}y}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aae311f3-6d07-5104-aa55-5d76b986510a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal CDF Using a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189c31de-c0e9-57cf-bbac-10e821864de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526555} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5f67cd-f071-54d3-9386-f77655d8f0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526625} {:answer/id #uuid "72442ffc-e56c-5203-94fd-73282d5c88c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526726} {:answer/id #uuid "de7e8486-049c-55a6-a95e-c6f6f07266c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526937} {:answer/id #uuid "f23d80ed-0301-53e6-88a5-ad22e0c8f2a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f5f67cd-f071-54d3-9386-f77655d8f0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526625} :answer-field/id #uuid "39cb31e5-27ee-572d-af4c-9fd41cce71e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "449c39ae-346f-5e58-9576-63532dc73237" :question/math-academy-id "q-159347" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\times [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)).\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(\\frac{\\pi }{2},y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aae311f3-6d07-5104-aa55-5d76b986510a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal CDF Using a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0048fc20-bfd7-5647-9ed3-5e83e80f7855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526497} {:answer/id #uuid "4760e0af-7c17-59e4-9428-b7e2b3770e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{log}_{2}⁡(2-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526641} {:answer/id #uuid "7e75a625-c85d-5866-9506-f4004e7988a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526753} {:answer/id #uuid "a6923305-e2d1-5771-8647-2ea06b7c3f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526821} {:answer/id #uuid "dd831f4c-c232-5259-91e3-6a8e05498594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{log}_{2}⁡(2-{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0048fc20-bfd7-5647-9ed3-5e83e80f7855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526497} :answer-field/id #uuid "118ed423-ef91-5a50-ac0b-83fc741088e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "431dc348-674b-5da2-8e50-c9106a497552" :question/math-academy-id "q-159349" :question/problem "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={log}_{2}⁡(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(1,y) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(1+(1){y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
